A test that failed once a day at midnight, and four things the owner asked for

**The CI failure, which was not the diff it landed on.** Four test files computed
the business day once at module load and asserted against it. A suite starting
before midnight in Asia/Jerusalem and reaching those tests after it writes into
one day and reads the other, so three counts come back 0 with nothing in the
message pointing at a clock — and everything passes on a re-run. It went red on
a push whose changes cannot explain it, which is the expensive part: the session
reads an unrelated diff. `frozen-business-day.test.ts` now fails any test that
freezes today at import.

A test that fails once a day, in the hour the people here actually work, is
worse than one that fails always: it teaches everybody to re-run instead of read,
and then a real failure gets re-run too.

**The header sat a few pixels off the content, and it is the scrollbar.** `html`
reserves the gutter permanently, so the page is laid out inside the viewport
minus the scrollbar — but a fixed element's containing block is the viewport
itself. Both centre the same container around two different midpoints. The
header is now inset by a registered `--scrollbar-width`; registered, because an
unregistered custom property substitutes as text and its `100%` would re-resolve
inside the fixed element and compute to zero.

**And the copy.** "לפתיחת פנייה" — the two earlier labels both named a recipient
("לפלטפורמה", "לצוות"), which was the wrong half of the sentence. The
placeholders say what the fields are instead of coaching someone already typing,
and both now show the characters left, because `maxlength` alone stops the
typing without ever saying why.

